    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0326" n="212" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg212"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R296/5151: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Fisher</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-03T12:00:00-05:00">October 3,
                    1931—noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>31. Your 39, October 1, 3 p.m.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d163-f1"/> I desire that you
                    seek an early audience with Mr. Venizelos and that you convey to him an
                    expression of this Government’s satisfaction that Greece and Bulgaria are
                    endeavoring to arrive at a mutually satisfactory arrangement respecting their
                    obligations during the debt suspension year.</p>
                <p>You should add that while this Government is of course not in a position to
                    formulate an opinion respecting the legal aspects of the question at issue, it
                    considers that the proposal understood to have been made by the Bulgarian
                    Government offers a practicable basis for a solution of the problem now
                    confronting the two countries and sincerely hopes that the Greek Government will
                    give that proposal its earnest consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
